 {"id":"RELEASE-002","status":"pending","priority":"P1","type":"bug","complexity":2,"title":"P1 [releng] remote tags v0.1.0 and v1.0.0 point at commits unreachable from main (pi-agent-migration / speclang-selfhosting)","reasoning":"From git ls-remote origin: v0.1.0 peels to 676795fcfb25df99c97dae6f204774cf8a3c5e3f and v1.0.0 peels to 2f2639a87a2615d6c5e232c83943ac6c871385e0. Neither satisfies git merge-base --is-ancestor against the release-authority tip b9bb5042; git branch -a --contains 2f2639a8 shows only origin/pi-agent-migration and origin/speclang-selfhosting. The local tags under the same names peel to different commits (v0.1.0 -> ff96a2e9, v1.0.0 -> f7b4ce7d) that ARE ancestors of main, so local and remote tag identity disagree by construction. Consequence for release work: a consumer who checks out v1.0.0 from GitHub receives code that was never merged into main, the stale lineages carry 992 and 964 commits not reachable from main, and no main-lineage baseline exists for a commit census except the local peel f7b4ce7d. This blocks the release baseline definition independently of code readiness. Doctrine forbids force-moving an existing tag, so the repair is a documented supersession on the main lineage rather than a re-point. Secret scans over origin/main..master, origin/main..speclang-selfhosting and origin/main..pi-agent-migration with gitleaks (redacted) returned 0 findings each, so the exposure is stale lineage, not leaked credentials.","acceptance_criteria":"From a fresh clone of dexdat/SpecLang, the peeled refs reported by git ls-remote --tags origin for v0.1.0 and v1.0.0 must each pass git merge-base --is-ancestor <peel> origin/main, OR the record must state that a new main-lineage release tag supersedes them and cite the divergence table. No existing tag may be deleted or force-moved to achieve this.","detail":{"metadata":{},"fingerprint":"bfc21f85295156bd307a94361d2ca3107a8212460cb70f42a4ed078127d0b3d9"},"capability_tags":["releng","tag-integrity"],"created_at":"2026-11-16T11:27:08.399501+00:00","updated_at":"2026-11-17 18:24:47.798245","foreman_note":"PM-VERIFY 2026-11-17T10:38Z (speclang-pm-2026-11-17-10-01-06, second pass) \u2014 premise RE-CONFIRMED live, and the tag map is now measured in BOTH directions. Published refs: `git ls-remote --tags origin` gives v0.1.0^{} = 676795fc and v1.0.0^{} = 2f2639a8; `git merge-base --is-ancestor <peel> origin/main` exits 1 for both (origin/main = 59b7b26f) \u2014 still unreachable, row stays open. NEW measurement: the LOCAL tag refs in this checkout disagree with the published ones \u2014 refs/tags/v0.1.0 -> ff96a2e9 and refs/tags/v1.0.0 -> f7b4ce7d, and both of those ARE ancestors of origin/main. The annotated tag objects differ too (local 3acb544b vs remote 3d823fc1 for v0.1.0; both carry tagger date 2026-03-22 15:31:37). Consequence for the fix direction: reconciling only the local map is a FALSE CLOSE, because `git describe` here reports v1.0.0-858-ga02736a1 while no consumer of the published repo can see that lineage, and a release cut from this checkout would be refused (tag exists) or force-move a published tag. Scope the repair to the PUBLISHED map (owner-gated: moving published tags needs force-push consent) and treat the local shadow tags as part of the same repair, not as evidence the tags are healthy.","deferred":true,"defer_reason":"ROUTED per SUPPORT-AGENT ROUTING: releng rows belong to the speclang-releng lane; cut/no-cut is releng decision and the CUT itself is owner-gated. Foreman wave SpecLang-foreman-2026-11-17-17-45-01 routes, not dispatches.","defer_review_by":"2026-11-24"}
